WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.720
"How many numbers contain a two?" and "How many twos are

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.330
written?" are different questions.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.330
Twenty-two is one number, but it contains two twos.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.780
Decide which you are counting, then count one place

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.570
at a time.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.160
Counting straight through a long list is

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.410
slow and error-prone.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.780
Counting each place separately is quick,

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.270
and repeated digits take care of themselves.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.450
All the whole numbers from one to one hundred

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:26.320
are written out.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.780
How many times is the digit seven written?

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.680
Pause and think about the units place first.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.790
In the units place, seven appears once in every ten

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.510
numbers: seven, seventeen, twenty-seven,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.460
up to ninety-seven.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:44.360
That is ten times.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.020
In the tens place, seven appears in seventy to

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.790
seventy-nine: ten times.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.190
Altogether the digit seven is written twenty times.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.490
Seventy-seven was counted once in each place,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.410
which is correct, because it has two sevens.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.420
Now the other question: how many numbers

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.370
contain a seven?

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.450
The column has ten numbers and the row has ten,

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.430
but seventy-seven is in both.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:11.390
Ten plus ten minus one is nineteen.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.090
A related skill is counting all the digits.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:19.560
Numbering pages one to ninety-nine uses nine digits for

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.620
the single-digit pages, and two digits each for the

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.650
ninety pages from ten to ninety-nine.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.770
That is nine plus one hundred and eighty:

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.050
one hundred and eighty-nine digits.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.580
Decide whether you are counting numbers or occurrences.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.480
Count each place separately.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:37.940
For numbers, remove any overlap.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.570
Notebook challenge: how many times is the digit three

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.700
written in the numbers from one to fifty?
